Diabetes Assignment – Question 1: Comprehensive Survival Analysis Guide
=========================================================================

This document summarizes the key concepts, methods, and models used to answer Question 1 of the diabetes assignment, 
along with simplified explanations, model assumptions, and illustrative code references.

---------------------------------------------------
1. Kaplan-Meier (KM) Model
---------------------------------------------------
- Purpose: Estimate the probability of survival over time from a specific event (e.g., diabetes diagnosis).
- Type: Non-parametric model (makes no assumption about distribution).
- Input: Time-to-event (futime_years), event indicator (status).
- Code: 
    km_fit <- survfit(Surv(futime_years, status) ~ 1, data = df)

- Assumptions:
  • Independent censoring
  • Event times are exact
  • Subjects are representative of the population

- What is censoring?
  Censoring means we don’t observe the outcome for some patients during the study period (e.g., patient dropped out or study ended before diabetes occurred).

- Example Interpretation:
  If KM shows a 5-year diabetes-free survival probability of 94.91%, then the estimated 5-year diabetes RISK is 100% - 94.91% = 5.09%.

---------------------------------------------------
2. Cox Proportional Hazards Model
---------------------------------------------------
- Purpose: Identify variables associated with the time to diabetes onset.
- Type: Semi-parametric (no assumption on baseline hazard).
- Code:
    cox_model <- coxph(Surv(futime_years, status) ~ age + male + weight + height + bmi + type, data = df_model)

- Assumptions:
  • Proportional hazards (HR is constant over time)
  • Linearity of covariates on the log-hazard
  • Independent censoring

- What is a Hazard?
  A hazard is the risk of the event occurring at a specific time, given survival until that time.
  Hazard Ratio (HR) > 1: Higher risk
  HR < 1: Lower risk
  HR = 1: No effect

- Model Output Example:
  type = 1 (treatment B) has HR = 1.39 → suggests 39% higher hazard than type A

---------------------------------------------------
3. Logistic Regression Model
---------------------------------------------------
- Purpose: Predict the probability of diabetes within 5 years (binary outcome).
- Code:
    df$diabetes_5y <- ifelse(df$futime_years <= 5 & df$status == 1, 1, 0)
    logit_model <- glm(diabetes_5y ~ age + male + weight + height + bmi + type, data = df_model, family = "binomial")

- Assumptions:
  • Linear relationship between log-odds and predictors
  • Independence of observations
  • Sufficient sample size

- Limitation:
  Logistic regression does not account for censoring or time-to-event. A person diagnosed at 3 years and another at 5.1 years are treated differently.

---------------------------------------------------
4. Parametric Survival Models (Optional Alternatives)
---------------------------------------------------
- Types: Weibull, Gompertz, Log-normal, Exponential
- Purpose: Estimate hazard and survival functions under distributional assumptions.
- Useful when the hazard function has a known shape (increasing/decreasing risk).

- Weibull Distribution:
  Flexible model used when hazard changes over time. Common in biomedical research.

- Gompertz:
  Often used in aging or mortality studies. Assumes exponential increase in hazard with age.

- Log-normal:
  Assumes the logarithm of survival time follows a normal distribution.

- Assumptions: Each has specific shape assumptions (e.g., constant, increasing, or decreasing hazard).

---------------------------------------------------
5. Additional Notes
---------------------------------------------------
- complete.cases() in R: removes rows with missing values in specified columns.

- Censoring: Must be considered in survival models like KM and Cox; not handled in logistic regression.

- Concordance Index (C-index): A measure of model’s ability to correctly rank event times; e.g., 0.76 means 76% correct order.

- Confidence Interval: Can be computed from survival models using summary(survfit(...))$surv and upper/lower bounds.

- Choosing Between Models:
  • KM: Good for describing time to event, no covariates.
  • Cox: Best for identifying risk factors, allows adjustment.
  • Logistic: Simpler but ignores time and censoring.
  • Parametric: Efficient if distributional assumptions are correct.

---------------------------------------------------
Summary of Best Practice:
---------------------------------------------------
Use KM for descriptive risk over time. Use Cox if covariates are important and data is censored. Use Logistic only when time-to-event is not available or important.